---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import BeforeAfter from '../../components/BeforeAfter.astro';
import FeatureCard from '../../components/FeatureCard.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../../components/SectionHeading.astro';
import AtAGlance from '../../components/AtAGlance.astro';
import InsightCallout from '../../components/InsightCallout.astro';

import headerImg from '../../assets/images/projects/deposify/header.png';
import beforeImg from '../../assets/images/projects/deposify/before.png';
import afterImg from '../../assets/images/projects/deposify/after.png';
import tenantImg from '../../assets/images/projects/deposify/tenant.png';
import depositDetailsImg from '../../assets/images/projects/deposify/deposit-details.png';
import dashboardImg from '../../assets/images/projects/deposify/dashboard.png';
import actionsImg from '../../assets/images/projects/deposify/actions.png';
import reportsImg from '../../assets/images/projects/deposify/reports.png';
import fundsImg from '../../assets/images/projects/deposify/funds.png';
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Deposify"
  description="Deposify had market validation, but its product story was hard to see: renters and property managers were sharing one interface built around two very different jobs. I helped split the product into clearer customer experiences, creating the redesign mockups the founders used to pitch and sell the company."
  tags={['B2B | B2C | SaaS', 'Product Strategy', 'Responsive Web']}
  heroImage={headerImg}
  slug="deposify"
  previousProject={{ title: 'LeadSuite', slug: 'leadsuite' }}
  nextProject={{ title: 'Unifying an Investigative Platform', slug: 'unified-platform' }}
>
  <div class="case-study-body">

    <!-- At a Glance -->
    <section class="case-section section">
      <div class="container">
        <SectionHeading title="At a Glance" />
        <AtAGlance
          role="Product Designer"
          team="1 PM, Director of Brand & Design, 3 Engineers"
          timeline="~4 months"
          outcomes={[
            { label: 'Acquired twice in 2022', text: 'Deposify was acquired twice, ultimately by Rhino in a multimillion-dollar deal. The redesign mockups were used as a primary pitch asset.' },
            { label: 'Business model made visible', text: 'Split a shared interface into two distinct experiences: a low-friction renter conversion flow and a scalable operations platform for property managers.' },
            { label: 'Design sold the vision', text: 'The client used the redesign mockups to pitch buyers, citing the designs as the main selling point alongside the technology.' },
          ]}
        />
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Challenge -->
    <section class="case-section section">
      <div class="container">
        <SectionHeading
          title="The Business Challenge"
          subtitle="Deposify had proven there was market demand for deposit management, but the product had not yet made the business easy to understand. Renters and property managers were sharing one interface, even though one group needed a fast payment path and the other needed an operational platform. The same UI was a ceiling on both adoption and buyer confidence."
        />
        <p class="design-question"><strong>Product Question:</strong> How do you make a validated product legible enough to scale, sell, and invest in?</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Making the Business Legible -->
    <section class="case-section section">
      <div class="container">
        <SectionHeading
          title="Making the Business Legible"
          subtitle="The redesign was not just a modernization effort. It clarified what Deposify actually was: a simple payment experience for renters on one side, and a multi-property financial operations platform for property managers on the other. That separation made the product easier for users to navigate and easier for buyers to understand."
        />
        <div class="insights-grid">
          <InsightCallout
            title="Renter conversion"
            description="Renters had one primary job: pay the deposit. Anything that made the interface feel like admin software created unnecessary friction at the point of conversion."
          />
          <InsightCallout
            title="Manager operations"
            description="Property managers needed a command center for units, payments, reporting, tasks, and bank accounts across properties, not a lightly expanded renter flow."
          />
          <InsightCallout
            title="Buyer confidence"
            description="Separating the experiences made the product vision easier to evaluate, showing both immediate user clarity and a path toward a larger operational platform."
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Before / After -->
    <section class="case-section section">
      <div class="container">
        <BeforeAfter
          beforeImage={beforeImg}
          afterImage={afterImg}
          beforeAlt="Original Deposify deposit management interface"
          afterAlt="Redesigned Deposify interface with streamlined deposit workflow"
        />
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Two Products in One -->
    <section class="case-section section">
      <div class="container">
        <SectionHeading
          title="Two Products in One"
          subtitle="The solution was to split the app into two distinct experiences, each designed from scratch for its user type. The property manager side became a full operational platform; the renter side became almost aggressively simple. That product split became the story the company could show to customers, investors, and eventual buyers."
        />
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Tenant experience -->
    <section class="case-section section">
      <div class="container">
        <SectionHeading
          title="The Renter Experience"
          subtitle="Renters have one job: pay their deposit. The redesign stripped everything back to make that as frictionless as possible. Rental agreement details and direct messaging to the property manager are available, but the primary screen is just a payment screen. The experience was designed for conversion, not administration."
        />
        <div class="features-grid">
          <FeatureCard image={tenantImg} title="Simplified Payment Screen" />
          <FeatureCard image={depositDetailsImg} title="Direct Messaging with Property Manager" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Property manager experience -->
    <section class="case-section section">
      <div class="container">
        <SectionHeading
          title="The Property Manager Experience"
          subtitle="Almost everything on the property manager side was net new, with little to reference in the existing product. The most demanding design problem was task management: a multi-property admin view that lets managers assign and track tasks across individual team members at each property. This side of the product had to communicate operational scale."
        />
        <div class="features-grid">
          <FeatureCard image={dashboardImg} title="Multi-Property Dashboard" />
          <FeatureCard image={actionsImg} title="Task Management Across Properties" />
          <FeatureCard image={reportsImg} title="Custom Reports" />
          <FeatureCard image={fundsImg} title="Bank Account Management" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Outcome -->
    <section class="case-section section">
      <div class="container">
        <SectionHeading
          title="The Outcome"
          subtitle="Development had just begun when the client told us they had sold the company. They had been using the redesign mockups to pitch potential buyers, and the designs were the main selling point alongside the technology. Deposify was acquired twice in 2022, ultimately by Rhino as part of a multimillion-dollar deal. Work made to improve a product became the visual case that made the business believable - twice."
        />
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Reflection -->
    <section class="case-section section">
      <div class="container">
        <SectionHeading title="Reflection" />
        <div class="reflections-grid">
          <div class="reflection-item">
            <h3>Question the assumption, not just the interface</h3>
            <p>The CTO had not made a design mistake. He had made a product assumption: that both user types needed the same experience. Redesigning the UI without questioning that would have missed the business problem entirely.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="reflection-item">
            <h3>Simplicity is also a design decision</h3>
            <p>The renter experience required as much thought as the property manager side. Deciding what to remove, what to hide, and what to surface as the single primary action is harder than designing a complex screen.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="reflection-item">
            <h3>Design communicates what a product could become</h3>
            <p>The mockups closing an acquisition they were not made to close was the clearest signal of the project: a well-designed product does not just show what it is, it shows what the business is capable of becoming.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

  </div>
</CaseStudyLayout>

<style>
  .features-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-2xl);
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .features-grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  import { initCaseStudyAnimations } from '../../scripts/scroll-animations';
  import { initScrollVelocityParallax } from '../../scripts/hover-effects';
  document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', () => {
    if (!window.location.pathname.startsWith('/projects/deposify')) return;
    initCaseStudyAnimations('.case-section');
    initScrollVelocityParallax();
  });
</script>
